  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Sunday) \nIssued at 1037 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nThe start of the week may very well bring a slight change in this \nmonotonous storm-filled pattern we've been in. The driving feature \ninvolved in this will be the upper level ridges we've been pinched \nbetween for the last week. This has kept the hotter temperatures at-\nbay, but led to more storms. Monday through Wednesday, we could \nstart to see more influence from the Bermuda ridge retrograding into \nthe southeast US. This would mean less (not zero) thunderstorm \ncoverage, but hotter and more humid conditions. Heat index values \nhave been in the upper 90s to low 100s while we've had all of these \nstorms. Monday through Wednesday afternoons could see us reach 105 \nin several places west of the Cumberland Plateau.\n\nAfter Wednesday, it's kind of a crap-shoot. Models diverge just \nenough to have fairly significant solution differences. GFS is \nleaning more towards a northwest flow aloft which would mean more \nthunderstorms for the second half of the week. The Euro says more \nupper ridging influences which would mean continued hotter \nconditions and less storms. At this point, the NBM has not chosen \na winner, so we'll have to work out the late-week details over the\nnext couple of days.",
